+## Subtopics
+
+### [Gradient Estimation via Weighted Least Squares Regression](23543-gradient-estimation-via-weighted-least-squares-regression.md)
+This method estimates the gradient of a scalar field at a query point by fitting a local polynomial function to data points in a spatial neighborhood using weighted least squares, then differentiating that function. It solves the problem of accurate gradient reconstruction in unstructured or irregular grids where standard finite difference stencils fail due to lack of connectivity or uniform spacing. Unlike its parent method, Feature-Aware Gradient Computation via Least Squares, which relies on explicit mesh connectivity to define the neighbor set, this method uses a spatial radius to define the neighborhood, making it "meshless". It is distinguished from simple averaging methods by its ability to handle low-quality meshes and high-frequency fields through adaptive weighting and polynomial fitting.
+
+### Linear Least Squares Feature Optimization
+This variant optimizes level-$l$ grid features $F_l$ by solving a linear least squares problem derived from the SDF prediction model [S2, unofficial]. It solves the normal equations to obtain the optimal $F_l$ and applies the resulting expression to update the features [S2, unofficial]. The formulation minimizes $\min_{F_l} \|h(x; F) - y\|_2^2 = \|r_{1:l-1}(x) + J F_l\|_2^2$ [S2, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Complexity:** Local computation per voxel. The matrix $(Q^T Q)$ can be precomputed if the neighborhood structure is uniform [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Preserves shape and density information better than standard finite differences; reduces fringing artifacts; feature-aware [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Preserves shape and density information of initial objects effectively with fewer control points [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** Requires solving a local least-squares problem; more complex than simple stencil methods [S1, unofficial].
 
